  "context_pill": "Capital Gains Computation under Section 48 and Section 55",
  "example_summary": "Mr. C sells a residential property acquired in 1975, requiring a computation of capital gains by applying the Section 55(2)(b) valuation cap and filtering pre-2001 improvement costs.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: Capital Gains Computation\n\n| Element | Statutory Provision | Core Condition & Threshold | Legal Treatment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Cost of Acquisition** | Section 55(2)(b) | For assets acquired before 1st April 2001, assessee can substitute Fair Market Value as on 1st April 2001. | Cannot exceed Stamp Duty Value as on 1st April 2001. |\n| **Cost of Improvement** | Section 55(1) | Expenses incurred prior to 1st April 2001 are completely ignored. | Only post 1st April 2001 improvement expenses are deductible. |\n| **Transfer Expenses** | Section 48 | Expenditure incurred wholly and exclusively in connection with the transfer. | Fully deductible from gross sale consideration. |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Valuation Cap Rule: Under Section 55(2)(b)(ii), if the fair market value of the capital asset as on 1st April 2001 exceeds the stamp duty value of the asset on that date, such stamp duty value shall be deemed to be the cost of acquisition.\n\n\n> [!WARNING] Pre-2001 Improvement Trap: Any capital expenditure incurred on additions or alterations prior to 1st April 2001 is ignored for computing the cost of improvement under Section 55(1).",
